{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob11re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Sam</strong>: Oh gosh, the exam about JavaScript Events is tomorrow and I just don't understand.<br />\n<strong> Philip</strong>:  JavaScript events? Those are easy! <br />\n<strong>Sam</strong>: They are not! I can't even understand what they are! <br />\n<strong> Philip</strong>:  They are not as complicated as you believe. Just think of them as any interaction between the user and the browser when they manipulate a page. <br />\n<strong>Sam</strong>: Manipulation of a page? <br />\n<strong> Philip</strong>:  Picture this; you always use your computer at night, right? You click a button to open a page, and it loads, there are two events there. First, you clicked somewhere in the page and second the page loaded because you manipulated to do so. </p>", "time": "0"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob11re2img02.jpg", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Sam</strong>: Ok, if that's an event then it's not as hard as I expected, but basically almost is an event! <br />\n<strong>Philip</strong>: Somehow, you are right. An event is virtually any response of the page to different actions. When you click something or when you fill a blank to submit any information; or when the mouse is over a button and it turns on, but as soon as you move the mouse, the button turns off. <br />\n<strong>Sam</strong>: So, does the user controls all the events? <br />\n</p>", "time": "41000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob11re2img03.jpg", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong> Philip</strong>: It's not usually as simple as the actions of users. Sometimes, the actions are produced by programs or browsers, like when the program recognizes that you are working offline and a popup notifies you about it; or when the program was downloading and it lets you know when it finishes; when a program has a failure and needs to be closed. <br />\n<strong>Sam</strong>: And how do they work? <br />\n<strong> Philip</strong>: It's simple. Developers use events to execute coded responses, so any interaction that the user or browser creates will cause a response from the program. There are many events, but you don't always need to learn them all. </p>", "time": "71000"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "_data": []}, {"id": 2, "template_override": 1, "type": "video", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n6le03ob11re5vqz01pt1", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "", "text": "Watch carefully the video.", "time": "0"}]}, "type": "video"}], "_data": []}, {"id": 3, "template_override": 1, "type": "video", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n6le03ob11re5vqz01pt2", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "", "text": "watch carefully the video.", "time": "0"}]}, "type": "video"}], "_data": []}, {"id": 4, "template_override": 1, "type": "video", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n6le03ob11re5vqz01pt3", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "", "text": "watch carefully the video.", "time": "0"}]}, "type": "video"}], "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "img_path": "", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can say what an event in JavaScript is.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can list some events in JavaScript.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about characteristics of some events in JavaScript.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What do you know about Javascript?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Have you seen a mobile application? What is your favorite app?", "type": "Fr